 "convention": {
  "semantics_posix": "A text file's line count is the number of newline-delimited records. For a file ending in a newline the count equals raw.count(b'\\n'); for a file with an unterminated final record, POSIX/wc adds 1 so that the partial last line counts. This is what `wc -l` implements.",
  "lines_posix_definition": "lines_posix = raw.count(b'\\n') + (0 if raw ends with b'\\n' else 1)",
  "old_convention_error": "r6 used raw.count(b'\\n') + 1 unconditionally. That is not wc -l and not a line count: it counts 'terminators' and then adds a line that does not exist. It is only correct for a file whose last record is unterminated, and is wrong by exactly +1 for every newline-terminated file.",
  "old_convention_expression": "lines_split_crlf = len(raw.decode('utf-8').split('\\n'))",
  "why_split_equals_old": "str.split('\\n') on text ending in '\\n' yields a trailing empty element, so len() is newline_count + 1 -- the same phantom line, reached by a different route. It is reported per file as lines_split_crlf purely so the +1 is visible; it is never used for a match decision.",
  "adopted_here": "lines_newline_count == raw.count(b'\\n')",
  "adopted_here_basis": "Every one of the 31 sources is newline-terminated, so lines_newline_count and lines_posix agree on this set; lines_newline_count is used because the declared column is a strict newline count and the two conventions must not be silently interchangeable. lines_posix is computed and reported so the third party can confirm the equivalence on this set.",
  "verdict_rule": "verdict == 'MATCH' only when bytes, lines and sha256 are all true; otherwise 'MISMATCH' with false_fields listing every false term.",
  "r6_verdict_rule_defect": "r6 set verdict from bytes+sha256 only, so lines_match=false never propagated into the printed verdict."
 },
